WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Press a fingertip and hold it.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.000
Is that one signal reaching your brain, or several?

3
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:13.410
In Touch and pain, you sorted four touch afferents of the hand into a two-by-two

4
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.730
table. You used firing alone.

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.280
So what lies behind each box?

6
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.620
And why split one touch into several signals at all?

7
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:27.350
Start simple. A smooth probe presses into a fingertip, holds still for a second,

8
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:28.750
then lifts.

9
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:34.503
Record from each kind of touch afferent in turn, and line up its spikes under the

10
00:00:34.503 --> 00:00:35.417
probe.

11
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:38.317
Which afferent keeps firing while the probe is held still?

12
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:47.017
The slowly adapting afferent that ends at Merkel cells.

13
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:50.577
It fires going in, and keeps firing through the hold.

14
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.187
It reports that something is there, and where its edges are.

15
00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:59.877
The rapidly adapting afferent in a Meissner corpuscle fires going in, and again on

16
00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:02.437
lifting. During the hold, it is quiet.

17
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.710
The Pacinian afferent is silent during the hold.

18
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.790
At most, it gives a brief burst as the probe goes in and lifts.

19
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.650
Now make the probe vibrate fast, with tiny movements.

20
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.470
Which afferent detects the smallest vibration?

21
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:23.633
The Pacinian afferent.

22
00:01:24.473 --> 00:01:26.533
It is the most sensitive to fast vibration.

23
00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:29.673
The slowly adapting afferent hardly responds.

24
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:32.887
Each class has a band where it is most sensitive.

25
00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:35.807
A band of best sensitivity, not a cutoff.

26
00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:39.077
Same skin, same probe, different records.

27
00:01:39.077 --> 00:01:41.337
Each is one part of the touch.

28
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.687
Here is the one idea to carry.

29
00:01:43.687 --> 00:01:49.587
Each class of low-threshold mechanoreceptor is most sensitive to a different part of

30
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:50.500
a touch.

31
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.080
Like overlapping filters, each class favours its own part.

32
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.280
A held press. Skin motion.

33
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.380
Fast vibration. The slightest bend of a hair.

34
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.220
A slow stroke.

35
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.280
The ending, with the neuron's own properties, shapes what a class responds to best.

36
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.320
Axon size and myelin set how fast the report travels.

37
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.800
Why split at all? The recorded classes are complementary.

38
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.520
One keeps reporting a held press.

39
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.380
Another catches fast vibrations too small for the rest.

40
00:02:23.983 --> 00:02:25.883
No class reports the whole touch.

41
00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:30.763
So the nervous system reads the pattern across the classes that share the skin.

42
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.417
This extends the Touch and pain rule.

43
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:37.977
Endings help decide what a line can report, even within one sense.

44
00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:40.477
One press, several reports.

45
00:02:41.517 --> 00:02:44.957
Next, mouse hairy skin, where three classes can share a single hair.
